      "created_at": "2023-05-01T20:56:31Z",
      "updated_at": "2023-05-01T20:56:31Z",
      "body": "To asnwer your first question, I would call it like you propose, using a lambda. For example let's suppose we've got a huey task:\r\n\r\n```\r\n@db_task\r\ndef work_on_item(item_id):\r\n    Item.objects.get(id=item_id).do_something()\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd a view that crates a task and works on it:\r\n\r\n```\r\ndef create_and_operate_item(request):\r\n    item = Item.objects.create()\r\n    transaction.on_commit(lambda: work_on_item(item.id))\r\n    render({})\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo, my understanding is that the `on_commit_task` \r\n* makes sure that the the decorated task will be called after the transaction finishes even if you don't use on_commit in your view\r\n* gives you access to the value from the task\r\n\r\nAbout *comfortably*  using Django's on_commit I think using the lambda is common practice.\r\n",
      "author_association": "NONE",
